  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The empirical findings and seasonal mediation pathways are derived from a single case study of Wuhan, which may limit their direct applicability to cities with different climatic or morphological characteristics.",
      "what": "This study constructed seasonal mediation models using remote sensing data from 2023 and 2024 in Wuhan, China, to analyze how impervious surface landscape metrics influence PM2.5 concentrations. It measures both the direct effects of these spatial patterns on air pollution and their indirect effects mediated through land surface temperature, while also accounting for the feedback loop of PM2.5 back onto surface temperatures.",
      "why": "It establishes that urban form metrics do not just correlate with air pollution directly, but actively alter local thermal environments which in turn amplify or mitigate PM2.5 levels, revealing a seasonal feedback loop that requires coordinated climate and pollution interventions."
    }
